     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. That is double the usual maximum of about 2,550 mg per day (2,000 mg per day for extended-release). The usual maximum for a single dose is 1,000 mg. An overdose like this raises the risk of lactic acidosis, which can be life-threatening, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **You're not a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed clinician with prescribing authority who is responsible for the patient. I can only enter orders on behalf of clinicians.\n\n- If this came from an existing order or chart, the prescriber should check it. It may be a typo, such as 850 mg or 1000 mg three times daily, or a total daily dose that was mistakenly entered as a single dose.\n- The prescribing clinician can confirm the intended dose and enter the order. They can also ask me to enter it once it's within safe limits.\n\nI can help with the non-clinical side, such as cost or utilization analysis of metformin orders."
      }
     ],
